  "news": [
    {
      "id": "news_1",
      "title": "Markets react to Fed's July interest rate decision",
      "source": "Yahoo Finance",
      "link": "https://finance.yahoo.com/economy/policy/articles/markets-react-feds-july-interest-210558874.html",
      "summary": "Markets react to Fed's July interest rate decision",
      "full_text": "On July 29, the Federal Reserve announced its decision on interest rates following its two-day policy meeting.\n\nAs expected, the central bank decided to keep the benchmark interest rate unchanged in the 3.50%-3.75% range.\n\nThough the Fed's newly appointed chair, Kevin Warsh, has emphasized the goal of lowering inflation to 2%, he hasn't outlined a detailed plan.\n\nEarlier, President Donald Trump called Warsh \"fantastic\" and reiterated that the Fed should lower the rates, \"He wants to do the right thing. I know what he wants to do.\"\n\nBut he sharply criticized other Fed officials as \"very political\" who \"perhaps have bad intentions.\" The president said,\n\n\"We should have the lowest interest rate in the world, like it used to be 30 years ago.\"\n\nHowever, the Warsh-led FOMC committee has decided to keep rates steady this time.\n\nFollowing the Federal Open Market Committee's (FOMC) latest interest rate decision, Bitcoin (BTC) and XRP were trading at $64,268.33 and $1.08 at the time of writing.",
      "published": "2026-07-29T21:05:58Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "news_2",
      "title": "Jim Cramer says this post-earnings sell-off is a golden buying opportunity",
      "source": "Yahoo Finance",
      "link": "https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/jim-cramer-says-post-earnings-221700892.html",
      "summary": "Jim Cramer says this post-earnings sell-off is a golden buying opportunity",
      "full_text": "Some stock drops look like bad news on the surface. Some are, yes, but some also tell a completely different story underneath.\n\nAnd I think American Express (AXP) investors have been handed the latter. The payments giant beat earnings expectations, raised its full-year revenue guidance, and reported its strongest card member spending growth in three years. The stock fell anyway.\n\nThis kind of reaction can frustrate shareholders and even confuse casual observers. But for \"Mad Money\" host Jim Cramer, it's a pattern he's seen before, and one he thinks is creating a clear opening.\n\nAmerican Express CEO Steve Squeri echoed that confidence in the company's Q2 2026 earnings statement, noting: \"Six months into the year, we're seeing stronger momentum than we expected.\"\n\nCramer's argument comes down to a single distinction. And it is one the market appears to have missed.\n\nAmerican Express beat earnings expectations in Q2 2026 and raised its full-year revenue growth guidance to approximately 10%, according to a company statement. Earnings per share (EPS) came in at $4.53, up 11% year-over-year (YoY). Net income reached $3.11 billion, up 8% from the same period a year earlier.\n\nThe answer lies in what management chose not to do. Instead of accelerating share repurchases, which would have boosted EPS more quickly, Squeri announced the company would reinvest its outperformance into growth initiatives.\n\nJim Cramer's net worth: How much does 'Mad Money's' stock-picking superhost make?",
      "published": "2026-07-29T22:17:00Z"
    },
